WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:04.573
Dying stars can go out with a bang, 

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:08.495
an enormous explosion called a supernova.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.172
These cosmic pyrotechnics shoot chemical elements

00:00:12.172 --> 00:00:15.326
and energetic particles out into space.

00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:18.464
And the explosion can release more energy

00:00:18.464 --> 00:00:21.356
than the sun will over its entire life.

00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:25.705
But scientists don t fully understand the extreme physics

00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:27.984
of supernova explosions.

00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:30.692
That's because there s nothing like them on Earth.

00:00:31.304 --> 00:00:32.697
Or... is there?

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:38.081
[MUSIC]

00:00:39.078 --> 00:00:41.001
Supernovas are fascinating.

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:43.345
They are not just distant explosions.

00:00:43.345 --> 00:00:46.669
They are the source of elements,  like the iron in our blood

00:00:46.669 --> 00:00:48.653
and the calcium in our bones.

00:00:48.813 --> 00:00:50.283
But they re far away.

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:53.039
So there's no hopping on a spacecraft to check
one out.

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:57.226
In our galaxy, supernovas only happen a few times a century,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.890
so spotting one nearby with a telescope is a waiting game.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.824
So scientists are using laboratory explosions

00:01:03.824 --> 00:01:06.804
to bring the physics of a supernova down to Earth.

00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:08.891
  Here s what we do know.

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:11.831
When a really big star gets old,

00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:13.784
it eventually runs out of fuel.

00:01:14.152 --> 00:01:16.556
Its core collapses and rebounds.

00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:19.328
That triggers a wave of high density and pressure

00:01:19.328 --> 00:01:21.670
that plows outward through the star.

00:01:21.792 --> 00:01:24.849
When this wave reaches the surface, the star explodes,

00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:25.718
[BOOM]

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:30.134
igniting a lightshow that could outshine its entire home galaxy.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.971
A mix of electrically charged particles, or plasma, 

00:01:34.143 --> 00:01:38.585
blasts into space at about 10,000 kilometers per second.

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:41.537
The explosion leaves behind a remnant,

00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:44.477
an expanding bubble of gas and plasma.

00:01:44.477 --> 00:01:46.086
Within the remnant itself,

00:01:46.086 --> 00:01:49.505
a shock wave forms and flings out charged particles 

00:01:49.505 --> 00:01:54.000
to energies that outdo even the biggest particle accelerators on Earth.

00:01:54.423 --> 00:01:55.452
And that remnant?

00:01:55.452 --> 00:01:58.874
It can stick around for hundreds of thousands of years.

00:01:59.415 --> 00:02:01.360
It's impossible to fully replicate 

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.086
the physics of a supernova in the laboratory.

00:02:04.308 --> 00:02:07.549
For one thing, a real supernova would blow
up everything in sight.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.408
But the same physics governs

00:02:09.408 --> 00:02:12.184
how charged particles flow in giant supernovas

00:02:12.184 --> 00:02:15.639
and in lab-made blasts that are shrunk down to a manageable size.

00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:18.859
Scientists also focus on one bit of the physics
at a time.

00:02:18.987 --> 00:02:20.090
They might try to reproduce

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.503
a supernova remnant's shock wave, for example,  

00:02:22.503 --> 00:02:24.467
but not all of the other complicated physics

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:26.005
that goes on during the explosion.

00:02:26.575 --> 00:02:28.274
If you want to understand supernovas,

00:02:28.274 --> 00:02:30.141
you might as well start big.

00:02:30.141 --> 00:02:32.034
The National Ignition Facility at

00:02:32.034 --> 00:02:34.931
Lawrence  Livermore National Laboratory in California,

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.000
has 192 laser beams that can momentarily unload

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.276
more power than the entire United States uses at a given instant.

00:02:44.902 --> 00:02:47.913
One team of physicists is using these lasers

00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:50.753
to study shock waves in supernova remnants.

00:02:51.118 --> 00:02:55.114
This kind of shock wave shows up when the blast's plasma collides

00:02:55.114 --> 00:02:59.100
with other plasma that's already hanging out in interstellar space.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:03.283
There, electric and magnetic fields grow stronger.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.594
These electromagnetic fields not only help form the shock wave, 

00:03:07.594 --> 00:03:12.000
but they also ping-pong charged particles back and forth across it.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:12.650
[WOOSH]

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.346
Each time the particles cross, they speed
up,

00:03:15.346 --> 00:03:17.940
eventually reaching extreme energies.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.010
But the details of how the magnetic fields
form

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.981
and how the particles are accelerated  have been a mystery.

00:03:25.436 --> 00:03:29.087
Scientists use lasers like those at the National
Ignition Facility 

00:03:29.087 --> 00:03:31.580
to produce colliding bursts of plasma,

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:34.755
revealing how magnetic fields form within.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:36.836
In a recent experiment,

00:03:36.836 --> 00:03:40.659
scientists also reproduced a supernova remnant's shock wave,

00:03:40.659 --> 00:03:44.040
showing for the first time how particles are accelerated.

00:03:44.738 --> 00:03:48.868
If you don t happen to have 192 laser beams at your disposal,

00:03:49.413 --> 00:03:52.466
good old-fashioned explosives can also do the trick.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.110
Researchers at Georgia Tech are focused on

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.594
re-creating the early moments of a supernova.

00:03:58.498 --> 00:03:59.900
Let s rewind.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:03.362
Before stars go supernova, they are layered like an onion,

00:04:03.362 --> 00:04:05.898
with heavier elements like iron in the center 

00:04:05.898 --> 00:04:09.255
and lighter elements like hydrogen on the outside.

00:04:09.441 --> 00:04:11.501
When the pressure wave inside the star

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.501
rams through the onion layers,

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:16.719
elements from the star's innards are ejected.

00:04:17.118 --> 00:04:18.915
To understand how those layers behave

00:04:18.915 --> 00:04:21.138
in the early moments of a supernova,

00:04:21.138 --> 00:04:25.393
the Georgia Tech team came up with a dynamite strategy:

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:30.754
Build a pizza slice-shaped blast chamber to mimic a wedge of a star,

00:04:30.754 --> 00:04:34.327
fill it with two layers of gas to mimic the onion structure,

00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:37.189
and add a powerful explosive.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.546
To capture the blast's aftermath,

00:04:39.546 --> 00:04:42.438
the researchers illuminate the gas with laser light.

00:04:43.304 --> 00:04:44.556
And they let er rip.

00:04:44.798 --> 00:04:45.939
[BOOM]

00:04:45.939 --> 00:04:48.356
As the resulting blast wave plows through,

00:04:48.741 --> 00:04:53.527
the gas layers form bubbles, spikes and mushroom-shaped tendrils.

00:04:53.527 --> 00:04:56.350
And some material flies outward.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:00.837
The experiments show that these kinds of
explosions can mimic a supernova.

00:05:00.837 --> 00:05:05.870
And they might help researchers

00:05:06.069 --> 00:05:09.777
And better understanding these early stages of a supernova

00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:13.384
could help researchers explain the complex structures

00:05:13.384 --> 00:05:15.635
that are seen later, in supernova remnants.

00:05:16.689 --> 00:05:18.708
Supernovas are some of the most extreme

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:20.075
environments in the cosmos.

00:05:20.075 --> 00:05:24.199
By studying them, we're exploring uncharted realms of physics.

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:27.063
Bringing supernovas down to Earth with experiments

00:05:27.063 --> 00:05:30.245
could help us understand other cosmic mysteries

00:05:30.245 --> 00:05:34.930
like how magnetic fields form in other realms of the universe.

00:05:35.784 --> 00:05:38.541
While the bang might spell the end of a star,

00:05:38.541 --> 00:05:40.350
it's just the beginning.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:43.972
The elements it releases go out to enrich the cosmos.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.880
The shock wave formed in the supernova remnant

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.022
could collapse clouds of gas,

00:05:49.022 --> 00:05:51.364
helping to form new stars.

00:05:51.549 --> 00:05:54.354
And energetic particles accelerated by that
shock wave

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.061
will streak across the sky,

00:05:57.061 --> 00:05:59.667
perhaps one day crash-landing on Earth.

00:06:00.263 --> 00:06:03.635
A supernova is the culmination of a star's life, 

00:06:04.034 --> 00:06:07.169
but it's the start of countless other stories.

